  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14096050/assets/a33571e46d18121fa46fce9cb0acdf9eb4c2a217aeaa19b36b9502bdc66772a6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $4\\text{\u00a0cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $26\\text{\u00a0cm}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{\u00a0cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=26\\text{\u00a0cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=26$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{\u00a0cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(26)^{2}\\cdot (-4)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\,112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $8\\,112\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=26\\,\\text{cm}.$",
